{"id":"SEC-001","category":"security","criterion":"input_validation","severity":"HIGH","score":3,"file":"head/.claude/scripts/red-test-check.sh","line":326,"code_snippet":"if [[ \"$RED_EXIT\" -eq 126 || \"$RED_EXIT\" -eq 127 ]]; then … finish NOT_A_RED 4","finding":"A test file absent at the red commit yields VERIFIED on the prescribed invocation (no --test-file): sh/python exit 2 and pytest exit 4 with file-not-found output are accepted as assertion failures.","reasoning_trace":"Read the red-side gate at :322-337 and NOT_A_RED_PATTERNS at :52-71; neither covers exit 2/4 or 'cannot open'/'No such file'. Built a fixture whose red commit predates the test; `--red <pre-test> -- sh tests/test_add.sh` printed 'sh: 0: cannot open tests/test_add.sh' and 'verdict: VERIFIED', exit 0. Both prescribed invocations (micro-sprint.md:43, reviewing-code/SKILL.md:219) omit --test-file.","critique":"The classifier accepts any non-0/126/127 exit as 'failed by assertion'.","remediation":"Add file-not-found shapes to NOT_A_RED_PATTERNS; gate sh/python exit 2 and pytest exit 4 when the output names a missing path; make --test-file mandatory in the prescribed invocations; add a default-path bats case expecting exit 4.","confidence":"high","references":["CWE-754 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"]}
{"id":"SEC-002","category":"security","criterion":"confidentiality_integrity","severity":"HIGH","score":3,"file":"head/.claude/scripts/red-test-check.sh","line":227,"code_snippet":"ln -s \"${MAIN}/${d}\" \"${wt}/${d}\"","finding":"Linked dependency directories (node_modules .venv vendor target .tox) make both runs resolve the live tree's source through workspace links or editable installs, so uncommitted state decides the verdict despite the header promise at :27.","reasoning_trace":"link_deps symlinks $MAIN/<dir> into each worktree by default. Fixture: node_modules/pkg -> ../src/pkg in the live tree, red commit with failing test + buggy src, green commit with no fix, fix only as an uncommitted edit. Result: red exit 0, green exit 0, 'verdict: DECOY' exit 3 although green holds no fix.","critique":"Source resolution through the link is not covered by the documented write-through caveat.","remediation":"Make linking opt-in or refuse to link a directory that resolves back into $MAIN (.pth/__editable__ scan for venvs; symlink entries resolving outside node_modules); add a workspace-link bats case; amend the header.","confidence":"high","references":["CWE-706 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/706.html"]}
{"id":"SEC-003","category":"security","criterion":"authorization","severity":"HIGH","score":2,"file":"head/.claude/skills/reviewing-code/SKILL.md","line":6,"code_snippet":"Bash(.claude/scripts/red-test-check.sh *)","finding":"The review role (deny_raw_shell: true) gains a wildcard grant on a script that execs the argv after -- verbatim (red-test-check.sh:250) and a second argv from --setup; the test command is taken from engineer-authored reviewer.md.","reasoning_trace":"Compared base and head allowed-tools and capabilities.execute_commands (:27 args: [\"*\"]). Read run_bounded: exec \"$@\" with cwd in a worktree, full env and uid. Reproduced `-- bash -c '…'` running in both worktrees. SKILL.md:219 instructs taking the red commit and test command from reviewer.md.","critique":"A single * grant on an argv runner removes the fence that keeps the review gate near read-only.","remediation":"Runner-specific allowlist patterns; drop --setup from the review grant; refuse inline-code interpreters and network fetchers as argv[0] unless --allow-shell; print the resolved argv in the verdict.","confidence":"medium","references":["CWE-269 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html","CWE-78 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html","OWASP ASVS V1.4 https://owasp.org/www-project-application-security-verification-standard/"]}
